            <ArticleTitle>[Non-medical therapy of myofascial pain].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>475-80</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">In a prospective study, the efficacy of manual therapy and dry needling was examined in 84 patients with chronic low back pain (mean duration of pain 4.4 years).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The initial value of pain on the visual analogue scale was about 6.6, the follow up value was reduced to 3.67 (p=0.0033).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Manual trigger point therapy of myofascial trigger points in the low back is effective.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
